in a month's time the American public  will go to the polls for the midterm  elections for many this is a referendum  on Joe Biden's first two years in the  White House and if the polls are to be  believed it won't be good news for the  Democrats what remains clear is that  American politics is still incredibly  divisive so what's at stake well in the  House of Representatives everything  435 congressional districts some are  solidly Republican some are solidly  Democrat but there are a few that can be  flipped and the Republicans know that a  net gain of just five seats will give  them control of the chamber that would  mean Nancy Pelosi is no longer the  speaker pushing through the president's  agenda she would be replaced by a  republican almost certainly Kevin  McCarthy in the Senate 35 of the 100  seats are up for grabs but only 10 of  those are thought to be competitive at  the moment both sides have 50 seats with  tie breaks decided by the casting vote  of the democratic vice president now  both sides know that a net gain of just  one will give them control of the  chamber  traditionally the president's party  doesn't do well in midterms Donald Trump  lost 40 House Seats Barack Obama lost 63  in his first midterm so what are the key  issues for voters first there's  inflation the cost of everything seems  to be going up and not just the basic  cost of foodstuffs and wages aren't  rising at the same level the economy is  most people's number one concern  there's also the cost of filling up your  vehicle it's expensive and with OPEC  announcing production Cuts it's going to  get more expensive just as people are  going to the polls so it's all bad news  for Democrats right well not necessarily  the Supreme Court's decision to overhaul  abortion rights in the United States has  led to more women and Democrats  registering to vote they believe the  only way to protect abortion is at The  Ballot Box and the Democrats also see  the polls that suggest Americans want to  restrict access to some weapons and they  believe that the only way to do that is  to give them a majority in the midterm  elections  if the polls are to be believed then the  Republicans could well win the house and  just fall short in the Senate but if  we've learned one thing over the last  six years it is that American politics  is highly unpredictable
 

